        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 79 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 79. District 79 serves students in temporary housing, hospitals, treatment centers, and detention facilities. Understanding the unique challenges these students face is crucial for effective advocacy.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            <p>Contact 311 for CSE details. When you contact 311, be prepared to provide specific information, including your child's date of birth, OSIS number (if known), and a clear description of your request (e.g., initial evaluation, annual review, re-evaluation). Document the date and time of your call, and the name of the 311 operator.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt (including the date, time, method of contact, and person contacted) and escalate to the NYC DOE. For District 79, persistence is key. Due to the transient nature of the student population, communication breakdowns can occur more frequently.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window for initial evaluations after parental consent) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.5(a).
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. Retain copies of all emails.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) - [Student Name]"</em>, as appropriate.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of contact, and a brief summary of each interaction. This log is crucial if you need to file a complaint.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Document the time and person contacted.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal or Program Director, if known). Request a read receipt.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Specifically mention that you are requesting a response according to IDEA regulations.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or timelines are violated. Instructions for filing a complaint are available on the NYSED website. See 8 NYCRR §200.5(l) for complaint procedures.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Advocacy Tips for District 79
    </h2>

    <div class="tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Given the unique circumstances of students in District 79, maintaining thorough records is paramount. This includes all communication, evaluations, IEPs, and any other relevant documentation.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Create a dedicated file (physical and/or digital) for all special education-related documents.</li>
                <li>Scan all hard copies of documents.</li>
                <li>Back up your files regularly.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Understand the IEP Process</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individualized Education Program (IEP) process, as outlined in the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State regulations (Part 200).
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review the NYSED "A Parent's Guide to Special Education" (available online).</li>
                <li>Understand your rights to participate in all IEP meetings.</li>
                <li>Know how to request specific services and accommodations.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Advocate for Appropriate Services</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Students in District 79 may have unique needs requiring specialized services. Be prepared to advocate for these services during the IEP process.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Consider the potential need for trauma-informed care.</li>
                <li>Advocate for small class sizes and individualized attention.</li>
                 <li>Explore the availability of assistive technology.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
		
		<div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you disagree with the district's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense.  The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate.  See 34 CFR § 300.502.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Request the IEE in writing, stating your disagreement with the district's evaluation.</li>
                <li>Inquire about the district's IEE provider list.</li>
                <li>If you choose a provider not on the list, be prepared to justify your choice.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if my child moves frequently within District 79?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Maintain consistent communication with the CSE and the new program as soon as your child transfers. Provide all relevant documentation (IEP, evaluations, etc.) to the new program to ensure continuity of services. Document all transfer dates and contacts. The district is obligated to implement the IEP to the greatest extent possible, even during transitions. According to IDEA, the IEP must be in effect at the beginning of each school year. This can be particularly challenging, but also especially important, for students with high mobility. </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the IEP developed by the CSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">You have several options, including: 1) Requesting an IEP facilitation meeting; 2) Filing a State Complaint with NYSED; 3) Requesting Mediation; 4) Requesting a Due Process Hearing. The "Prior Written Notice" (PWN) you receive after the IEP meeting should outline your rights. Document your objections in writing immediately after the meeting.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How can I get an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) for my child?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Submit a written request to the CSE stating that you disagree with the district's evaluation and are requesting an IEE at public expense. The district must then either fund the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend the appropriateness of their evaluation. You are entitled to an IEE if you disagree with an evaluation performed by the school district. 34 CFR § 300.502.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are my rights if my child is facing disciplinary action?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Students with IEPs have specific protections under IDEA regarding disciplinary actions, particularly suspensions exceeding 10 days. A manifestation determination review must be conducted to determine if the behavior is related to the child's disability. If it is, the IEP team must develop or revise a behavior intervention plan. 34 CFR § 300.530. Know your child's rights regarding suspension and expulsion. </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


    </div>
</main>
